## Founding & History
Puddleduck Wines was established in 1997 by Darren and Jackie Brown, who purchased a 15-acre property in Richmond, Tasmania. The first wines were crafted in 2003. In 2015, the winery unveiled a $550,000 makeover of its tasting facility, which included a playground and a deck overlooking the dam and vines. After 27 years, Darren and Jackie Brown announced the sale of Puddleduck Vineyard to Derwent Estate, with the ownership transition officially occurring on May 9, 2025. Puddleduck is expected to continue operating as a standalone business under the new ownership.

## People
Puddleduck Wines was founded and operated by Darren and Jackie Brown. Darren Brown had 30 years of experience in the Tasmanian wine industry as of 2015, having started at Moorilla Estate at the age of 17. As of May 9, 2025, the winery transitioned to the ownership of the Hanigan family, who also own Derwent Estate. The current winemaker is John Schuts, whose experience includes winemaking in Western Australia, Burgundy, and Derwent Estate. Andrew Hanigan, a fifth-generation family member of Derwent Estate, serves as the viticulturist, having worked in vineyards since he was 13. Ange Morgan is the General Manager. The winery is also known for its resident vineyard dog, Polly, and pet duck, Lucky.

## Vineyard & Site
Puddleduck Wines is located in Tasmania's Coal River Valley. The winery operates on a 15-acre property purchased in 1997. Puddleduck crafts wines entirely from estate-grown fruit, with all grapes hand-harvested, pruned, and hand-labelled. The vineyard produces small-batch, cool-climate wines. Varietals planted include Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, and Riesling. As of 2015, several hectares of new vines had been planted on the hillside overlooking the cellar door. All grapes are grown on-site, and the wine is made on-site in a small tin shed. The vineyard area is approximately 3.5 hectares.

## Cellar Door Experience
The Puddleduck Wines cellar door is open daily from 10 am to 5 pm, except for some public holidays. Visitors can choose from various tasting options, including a quick bar tasting, a seated tasting paddle, or a premium tasting in the indoor tasting room, which overlooks the duck pond and vineyard. Structured sit-down tastings, sometimes accompanied by a cheese platter, are also available. Bookings are recommended for all tasting experiences, particularly for larger groups. The facility includes a playground and a deck overlooking the dam and vines, which were part of a $550,000 renovation in 2015. Vineyard and winery tours are also offered. The cellar door experience is often enhanced by the presence of the winery's pet duck, Lucky, and vineyard dog, Polly. There is also an "Everything Ducky Duck Shop" selling duck-themed items and local provisions.

## Food & Hospitality
Puddleduck Wines offers a selection of local cheeses to complement its wines. An all-day grazing menu is available. The winery has a unique "Reverse BYO®" concept, where visitors are encouraged to bring their own food to enjoy in the outdoor areas, while Puddleduck provides the wine. The "Pecking Menu" features locally sourced produce. The winery also offers other drinks, including local beers, ciders, and spirits. Weekend music sessions on the deck were planned as of 2015. For accommodation, Puddleduck offers the "Pinot Cottage," a secluded, self-contained timber cottage with two bedrooms, a full European kitchen, a spa, and a wood fire.

## Awards & Recognition
Puddleduck Wines has received recognition, including being listed as the #2 attraction in the Richmond area on TripAdvisor as of 2015. The 2012 release of their Bubbleduck sparkling wine was described as "outstanding," and the 2015 Riesling was noted as a "standout". The Real Review has provided rankings for several Puddleduck wines, such as the Bubbleduck Sparkling 2016, which was ranked #15 of 15 2016 Sparkling whites from Tasmania. The Puddleduck Fumé Blanc Sauvignon Blanc 2014 was ranked #11 of 28 2014 Sauvignon Blanc from Tasmania. The Puddleduck Pinot Noir 2018 was ranked #68 of 114 2018 Pinot Noir from Tasmania, and the Puddleduck Rosé 2015 was ranked #52 of 111 2015 Rosés from Australia.

## Sustainability & Certifications
Puddleduck Wines maintains a strong focus on sustainability. The winery operates as an energy-neutral facility. It holds Sustainable Tourism Accreditation by ATIC.

## Visiting Practical Info
Puddleduck Wines is located at 992 Richmond Road, Richmond, Tasmania, 7025, Australia. It is a short drive from Hobart and approximately a five-minute drive from the historic township of Richmond. The phone number for Puddleduck Wines is (03) 6260 2301. Their website is www.puddleduck.com.au. The winery also has a presence on Facebook and Instagram. Bookings can be made via web, email, or phone.

## Notable / Distinctive
Puddleduck Wines is genuinely distinctive for its "Reverse BYO®" policy, which allows visitors to bring their own picnic food to enjoy on the grounds while purchasing wine from the cellar door. The winery is also known for its friendly pet duck, Lucky, and vineyard dog, Polly, who often greet visitors. A unique event hosted by the winery is the bi-annual Puddleduck Dunk Charity Duck Race, which raises funds for breast, prostate, and childhood cancer research. Puddleduck is one of the few Australian vineyards that handles every aspect of wine production on-site, from growing the fruit to crafting and selling the wines. For a period, Puddleduck wines could only be purchased at the cellar door or online, and were exclusively featured on one wine list, at Henry's at the Henry Jones Hotel in Hobart.

